**My vote is a qualified yes.** The strongest connection is to the repo’s accounting for how a factoring algorithm acquires relations. The paper gives an exact example where charging separately for every apparent query overprices the computation. That makes hatch H and hatch R worth reconsidering. The decisive question is whether a factoring algorithm can compute a few aggregate checks *before it knows which candidates are useful*.

## D1. The strongest transfers

**1. Exact repair suggests a branch selector for quadratic CRT decoding.** In Theorem 4.1, shifting the rows makes the count binomial at each binary frequency. A degree \(O(\sqrt{m\log m})\) polynomial gets parity exactly right in the central window. The crucial detail is that the eigenvalue tuple tells the circuit which rows can be wrong. It sends \(2s\) linear checks into the common eigenbasis for at most \(s\) exceptional rows per frequency. The exceptional set can change at every frequency. Section 5 then supplies the equally important algorithmic ingredient: it shares the work of applying the predictor. [Paper, §§4–5](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-logarithmic-exponent-of-finite-Fourier-transforms-November-24-2026/paper.pdf)

The closest factoring analogue is M2, the repo’s search for a smooth value of a public quadratic form \(Q(u,v)\). At each good split prime, the two possible projective roots are public. A primitive smooth witness chooses one root at each prime dividing it. Once enough of those choices are known, rational reconstruction recovers \(u/v\). **The missing step is selecting the primes themselves.** Even an oracle that handled all root orientations for free would leave the existing traversal enumerating \(L_N[1-\beta,2\nu]\) divisor products in a box of side \(L_N[1-\beta,\nu]\). Fixed-weight Johnson certificates already fail at leading order. A genuine transfer would compute checks from the public form that steer the search toward a productive branch before paying that enumeration cost. The paper gives erasure repair with known locations. M2 needs location information as part of the answer. [M2 construction and traversal ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/report.md) · [CRT decoding barriers](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/crtdecode_results.md:27)

The payoff is unusually clean. With \(n=L_N[\beta,\kappa+o(1)]\) factor-base primes, one useful witness per \(n^a\) charged work at constant success probability would give the M2 heuristic cost
\[
L_N[\beta,\max\{(1+a)\kappa,2\kappa\}],
\]
assuming adequate independent relations and fruitful class-group extraction. Taking \(\beta=1/4\) crosses the target. The distinction between M2 and the single-interval constructor matters here. Even an ideal constructor for the repo’s single-interval Prop SI improves only the \(1/3\) constant. [M2 ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/report.md:55) · [Prop SI](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/suffmap_results.md:318)

**2. Hatch R has an actual binary structure on which to try the idea.** In the repo’s multiquadratic tower, above a completely split prime the subfield ideal-parity indicators span \(RM(1,r)\), of dimension \(r+1\) on \(2^r\) coordinates. This suggests a precise target. Write the actual valuation-parity matrix on \(h\) candidate relations as \(A=C+E\), where \(C\) records the predictable subfield contributions. If \(\operatorname{rank}C+\operatorname{rank}E<h\), there is a dependency. Per-column sparsity of \(E\) alone buys nothing here: the identity matrix has one exception per column. The transfer would be to arrange that the exceptional contributions across many rows can *jointly* be recovered and canceled cheaply. The paper’s binary Fourier circuit operates with 2 invertible, so its gates cannot simply be applied to these mod-2 valuations. [Tied parity and the thin-dependency ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hatch_results.md:87)

Under the repo’s thin-dependency heuristic, a constructible, fruitful family of \(h=L_N[\eta]\) rows gives \(L_N[\max(1/5,\eta)]\) RAM factoring. The missing arithmetic is a family for **one** \(N\) with small lifts and norms, controlled exceptional parity, and a dependency that splits \(N\). This is where reuse meets the profile hatch \(\mathrm{LP}_{\rm exc}^{\rm rel}\). Computing the idealized code faster leaves all three obligations outstanding. [Hatch results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hatch_results.md:91)

**3. The rectangle test could strengthen the barrier itself.** The repo can sometimes confine exceptionally small norms to a few proper subspaces, yet cannot bound the fresh parity rank contributed *inside* those subspaces. Part I suggests examining the matrix of candidates versus prime-ideal parities after quotienting out previously exposed rows, tracking its support and rank loss together. The paper even shows how to localize a rank correction that mixes independent blocks. An arithmetic counterpart could charge a shared descent DAG for the fresh revelations needed to sustain its rank, strengthening the model’s \(1/3\) floor. The missing lemma must connect valuation entries to actual stripped cofactors. The repo’s \(q^3b_i\) parents and \(qb_0\) eliminator illustrate why: parity cancellation leaves \(q^2\) in the residual. The Fourier rectangle theorem supplies no such arithmetic inequality. [Paper, §§2–3](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-logarithmic-exponent-of-finite-Fourier-transforms-November-24-2026/paper.pdf) · [Open rank-to-residual lemma](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hatch_results.md:132)

**4. Theorem T identifies exactly what Fourier repair would have to produce.** For \(r=\operatorname{ord}_N(a)\), the Fourier coefficient of \(g(a^t)\) at \(j/r\) is the sum of the multiplicative-character coefficients of \(g\) whose restrictions to \(\langle a\rangle\) equal that tone. A speculative target is a batch of publicly evaluable observables whose repaired combination has squared peak mass at least \(1/L_N[\beta,O(1)]\), with reduced denominator \(d\) satisfying \(r/d\le L_N[\beta,O(1)]\). With bounded, affordable sample access and adequate success over bases, sparse Fourier recovery and testing multiples of \(d\) would fit \(L_N[\beta,O(1)]\). The missing object is the observable or its repair checks. Installing the unknown character phases in the repair table assumes precisely the information being sought. A faster dense transform leaves the coefficients and the cost of supplying its samples unchanged. [Theorem T and its access conditions](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/periodfind_results.md:120)

## D2. Where the skeptical argument is too quick

Factoring algorithms *also* choose auxiliary primes. Section 7.2 finds many prime axes \(p_i\le B\) with \(\log\operatorname{lcm}_i(p_i-1)=O(B^{4/5}\log B)\). Rader ordering and the common extension let one representation serve many local operations. The fair question is whether a deliberately chosen **factor base** could likewise support cheap joint checks while retaining enough smooth norms. That would require adequate splitting and witness density for the particular discriminant, plus a way to exploit the common representation during selection. Bombieri–Vinogradov in §7.2 establishes neither. Applying the construction to the hidden prime divisor would additionally require control of its group order. [Paper, §§7.2, 7.4](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-logarithmic-exponent-of-finite-Fourier-transforms-November-24-2026/paper.pdf)

I agree with Claude on direct substitution. At length \(D=L_N[\gamma,c]\), saving a power of \(\log D\) preserves both \(\gamma\) and \(c\). Even granting a uniform bit implementation, this leaves the Pollard–Strassen and batch-smoothness ledgers intact. Generic sparse NFS linear algebra has no demonstrated Fourier structure here. The historical lesson is narrower and more interesting: earlier work had already beaten \(D\log D\) in unrestricted models. This paper identifies the optimal logarithmic exponent and shows how independent slices *inside one instance* can supply the batch. That is the precedent for seeking single-\(N\) reuse. [Paper, §§1.2–1.3](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-logarithmic-exponent-of-finite-Fourier-transforms-November-24-2026/paper.pdf)

## D3. Odds and one follow-up

**My subjective probability is 0.2%** that this paper’s ideas lead to a credible classical general-\(N\) factoring algorithm with fixed \(\alpha<1/3\), counting a heuristic algorithm.

I would spend the next experiment on **one public aggregate check for M2**. Bisect each quadratic-form box. Rank equal-area children by the excess, over its local-density prediction, of
\[
\sum_{\substack{(u,v)\in C\\\gcd(u,v)=1}}\binom{\omega_B(Q(u,v))}{2},
\]
where \(\omega_B\) counts distinct good split prime divisors up to \(B\). This check has an independent implementation by summing lattice-point counts for *pairs* of prime-root conditions. It can therefore be tested without first locating a smooth witness. Use held-out forms near the one-witness threshold, verify leaves exactly, and charge every lattice count, primitivity correction, and failed branch. Compare total work per fresh verified relation against the matched divisor sieve. The result worth pursuing would be a growing reduction in **branches visited per relation** large enough to pay for the checks. That measures the one thing the existing reconstruction speedup never touched. [Existing matched control and measurements](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/hatch_h1c/report.md:39)